    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d37">

                <pb facs="0086" n="74" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg74"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-19T17:00:00-04:00">July 19, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5954. From Cramer<anchor n="31" xml:id="d37-f1"/> FEA for Blaisdell.<anchor
                        n="32" xml:id="d37-f2"/> Cox<anchor n="33" xml:id="d37-f3"/> discussed with
                    Robert Brand before latter’s departure for London FEA’s wish that British
                    carefully reconsider their previous objections to giving under Reverse
                    Lend-Lease all Suez Canal tolls paid by US. British have been told that FEA
                    would be agreeable if British secured participation by French. Records not
                    complete but indicate around 11½ million dollars expended by US from prior to
                    1943 through 1944 with probability that 1945 charges will exceed 6 million.
                    Please give your strong support wherever appropriate. [Cramer.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d37-f1" n="31" xml:id="d37-f1-content">
                <p>Ambrose C. Cramer, Acting Director, British Empire and Middle East Branch,
                    Foreign Economic Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d37-f2" n="32" xml:id="d37-f2-content">
                <p>Thomas C. Blaisdell, Chief of the United States Mission for Economic Affairs,
                    London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d37-f3" n="33" xml:id="d37-f3-content">
                <p>Oscar Cox, Deputy Administrator, Foreign Economic Administration.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
